egislative achievement  the curtis act is seen by many native  peoples  as a betrayal kent blanson is a  professor of indigenous studies and  history at the university of kansas  what it was was to strip native lands  treaty lands  from native nations and to bring us as  native peoples into  the capitalist economic system and part  of this too was a greater design of  assimilation and acculturation  the curtis act ended up stripping rights  from native americans  including curtis's own car nation it was  a way to protect his people  as how he saw it or how he envisioned it  now we have the benefit of hindsight um  you know in the present to be able to  say  that's a horrible decision and even  later on he would regret the decision  while curtis left behind a complicated  legacy  he never tried to distance himself from  his indigenous  roots he faced many uphill battles in  some ways he wouldn't be allowed in  certain restaurants in oklahoma but he  would eventually be a senator and vice  president  listen to what else he accomplished he  proposed  the very first version of the era after  being  one of the first supporters of the 19th  amendment he  uh was a sharpener or sort of shaper  of the gold standard act he he was known  as being you know the consummate  politician because he kept index cards  exactly on every single one of the  constituents that he met  so he really brought home a lot to the  people he's a  fascinating piece of history it really  is undertold  story obviously
 

